WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.207 --> 00:00:02.810
(ominous guitar music)

00:00:02.810 --> 00:00:04.950
- [Nahre] Goth is an entire
subculture which inspired

00:00:04.950 --> 00:00:06.590
not only decades of music,

00:00:06.590 --> 00:00:07.927
but fashion and films.

00:00:07.927 --> 00:00:10.090
(ominous guitar music)

00:00:10.090 --> 00:00:13.110
- In fact, the unique look
associated with goth culture

00:00:13.110 --> 00:00:15.870
is as much a part of the
brand as is melancholy music.

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:17.903
Where country has its
belt buckles and boots,

00:00:17.903 --> 00:00:20.530
goth has its tripp pants and chains,

00:00:20.530 --> 00:00:22.270
but we wanna know what came first.

00:00:22.270 --> 00:00:24.843
Was it the moody fashion
or was it the music?

00:00:24.843 --> 00:00:28.010
(upbeat techno music)

00:00:28.990 --> 00:00:31.290
- So we're not talking about
the Hot Topic mall goths.

00:00:31.290 --> 00:00:32.740
This was something that came straight out

00:00:32.740 --> 00:00:34.840
of the late '70s post-punk scene.

00:00:34.840 --> 00:00:37.550
It was the dark, moody black
lipstick wearing specter

00:00:37.550 --> 00:00:38.970
known as goth rock.

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:40.900
- Goth's influence can be seen everywhere,

00:00:40.900 --> 00:00:43.080
from Tim Burton movies to Billie Eilish.

00:00:43.080 --> 00:00:44.634
There's even an annual goth cruise.

00:00:44.634 --> 00:00:46.450
(foghorn blowing)
(seagulls cawing)

00:00:46.450 --> 00:00:48.840
- Today, LA and I are going
to embrace our inner goths,

00:00:48.840 --> 00:00:50.950
put on the black mascara and lipstick,

00:00:50.950 --> 00:00:53.550
and create an appropriately moody song,

00:00:53.550 --> 00:00:54.940
but first we're going to talk about

00:00:54.940 --> 00:00:56.760
where this sub-culture really started.

00:00:56.760 --> 00:00:59.290
So, aside from the silver
jewelry and black jeans,

00:00:59.290 --> 00:01:01.020
what exactly is goth?

00:01:01.020 --> 00:01:03.070
Firstly, we need to get into the vibe

00:01:03.070 --> 00:01:04.740
by reading Edgar Allan Poe.

00:01:04.740 --> 00:01:05.573
Sorry.

00:01:05.573 --> 00:01:06.503
Let's look it up now.

00:01:07.537 --> 00:01:09.597
"No doubt I now grew very pale,

00:01:09.597 --> 00:01:12.807
"but I talked more fluently
and with a heightened voice."

00:01:12.807 --> 00:01:13.837
- "Villians, I shrieked.

00:01:13.837 --> 00:01:16.287
"Dissemble no more, I admit the deed.

00:01:16.287 --> 00:01:17.267
"Tear the planks here,

00:01:17.267 --> 00:01:19.979
"here is the beating
of this hideous heart."

00:01:19.979 --> 00:01:20.940
Oh my goodness.

00:01:20.940 --> 00:01:22.900
- That's dark.

00:01:22.900 --> 00:01:24.780
- [LA] While the term
Gothic originally came about

00:01:24.780 --> 00:01:27.420
to describe European
architecture of the Middle Ages,

00:01:27.420 --> 00:01:29.800
from roughly the 12th
Century through the 16th,

00:01:29.800 --> 00:01:31.220
the term was later used to describe

00:01:31.220 --> 00:01:34.113
romantic and spooky literature
from the 1700s and 1800s.

00:01:34.950 --> 00:01:37.850
- So what does that have to do
with the moody musical style?

00:01:37.850 --> 00:01:40.130
Most give credit to music
critic John Stickney

00:01:40.130 --> 00:01:41.940
for connecting the term with the music.

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:43.870
After meeting Jim Morrison of The Doors

00:01:43.870 --> 00:01:46.580
in a gloomy wine cellar in 1967,

00:01:46.580 --> 00:01:48.520
he wrote that it was the perfect room

00:01:48.520 --> 00:01:50.920
to honor the gothic rock of The Doors,

00:01:50.920 --> 00:01:52.460
referencing the somber lyrics

00:01:52.460 --> 00:01:54.902
and dark musical stylings of the band.

00:01:54.902 --> 00:01:57.097
♪ This is the end ♪

00:01:57.097 --> 00:01:59.490
But by the late '70s and early '80s,

00:01:59.490 --> 00:02:01.200
goth rock was a genre.

00:02:01.200 --> 00:02:02.680
- Where did goth rock come from?

00:02:02.680 --> 00:02:04.090
It's considered to be an offshoot

00:02:04.090 --> 00:02:07.160
of the late '70s British punk
rock scene called post-punk.

00:02:07.160 --> 00:02:09.940
Post-punk took the energy
and the do-it-yourself spirit

00:02:09.940 --> 00:02:13.160
of punk in bold,
experimental new directions,

00:02:13.160 --> 00:02:15.360
blending punk with
influences from other genres

00:02:15.360 --> 00:02:18.380
like disco, funk, and electronic music.

00:02:18.380 --> 00:02:20.700
While punk was considered
an angry rebel yell

00:02:20.700 --> 00:02:23.410
at the establishment,
goth became the opposite,

00:02:23.410 --> 00:02:26.393
a fatalistic, romantic
resignation to the inevitable.

00:02:26.393 --> 00:02:27.680
(clock bell ringing)

00:02:27.680 --> 00:02:30.220
- During this time, three bands
really came to the forefront

00:02:30.220 --> 00:02:32.850
of the emerging genre
to become the pioneers

00:02:32.850 --> 00:02:34.800
of the goth rock movement,

00:02:34.800 --> 00:02:38.500
Joy Division, Siouxsie and
the Banshees, and Bauhaus.

00:02:38.500 --> 00:02:41.210
So what made these goth bands so gothic?

00:02:41.210 --> 00:02:44.170
- Goth music is often
identified by its dark themes

00:02:44.170 --> 00:02:46.520
and is considered nihilistic and poetic.

00:02:46.520 --> 00:02:49.830
A recurring heavy bass,
a moody synthesizer,

00:02:49.830 --> 00:02:51.690
and the use of echo doesn't hurt either.

00:02:51.690 --> 00:02:53.570
Perhaps what can be
considered as the archetype

00:02:53.570 --> 00:02:57.630
for all goth music was Bauhaus'
single, Bela Lugosi's Dead,

00:02:57.630 --> 00:03:00.290
which came out, and in
1979 as considered by many

00:03:00.290 --> 00:03:03.375
to be the first truly gothic rock song.

00:03:03.375 --> 00:03:06.097
♪ Bela Lugosi's dead ♪

00:03:06.097 --> 00:03:08.340
♪ The bats have left the bell tower ♪

00:03:08.340 --> 00:03:11.100
Bela Lugosi was an actor who
played the title character

00:03:11.100 --> 00:03:15.140
in Universal's 1931 film,
Dracula, and whose real life

00:03:15.140 --> 00:03:17.420
was almost as tragic as his fictional one.

00:03:17.420 --> 00:03:19.680
The band's bassist,
David J, said he came up

00:03:19.680 --> 00:03:21.270
with the idea for the song and wrote it

00:03:21.270 --> 00:03:24.340
after binge watching vampire
movies on television.

00:03:24.340 --> 00:03:25.830
The band's front man, Peter Murphy,

00:03:25.830 --> 00:03:27.710
said the sound of the song was in part

00:03:27.710 --> 00:03:29.500
also inspired by dub reggae.

00:03:29.500 --> 00:03:30.740
You can hear the influence.

00:03:30.740 --> 00:03:34.368
Listen to The Revolutionaries'
Reaction in Dub from 1978,

00:03:34.368 --> 00:03:37.200
(dub reggae music)

00:03:37.200 --> 00:03:38.818
and here's Bela Lugosi's Dead.

00:03:38.818 --> 00:03:41.818
(punk reggae music)

00:03:42.970 --> 00:03:45.880
Bauhaus' song may be
considered goth's first anthem,

00:03:45.880 --> 00:03:48.260
but it was just the beginning
for the musical genre.

00:03:48.260 --> 00:03:52.070
The English band Siouxsie and
the Banshees formed in 1976,

00:03:52.070 --> 00:03:54.740
taking their name from the
horror movie Cry of the Banshee,

00:03:54.740 --> 00:03:57.790
which starred another classic
of horror, Vincent Price.

00:03:57.790 --> 00:04:00.170
Some argue Siouxsie and
the Banshees was originally

00:04:00.170 --> 00:04:03.220
a punk band that transitioned
to post-punk goth

00:04:03.220 --> 00:04:05.454
with their fourth album, Juju, in 1981.

00:04:05.454 --> 00:04:08.127
♪ We are entranced, we are entranced ♪

00:04:08.127 --> 00:04:09.730
♪ Spellbound ♪

00:04:09.730 --> 00:04:12.150
But there's no debate that
lead singer Siouxsie Sioux

00:04:12.150 --> 00:04:14.330
heavily influenced goth fashion,

00:04:14.330 --> 00:04:15.610
but more on that later.

00:04:15.610 --> 00:04:18.520
- Other bands such as Joy
Division, the Sisters of Mercy,

00:04:18.520 --> 00:04:22.460
The Cure, The Damned, Dead
Can Dance, Type O Negative,

00:04:22.460 --> 00:04:25.620
and many more put on their capes
and came out of the shadows

00:04:25.620 --> 00:04:29.010
with their dark messages of
loneliness and rebellion.

00:04:29.010 --> 00:04:31.904
So what exactly are we
getting ourselves into?

00:04:31.904 --> 00:04:32.737
(giggling)

00:04:32.737 --> 00:04:35.602
- Listen, when I thought
about goth, initially,

00:04:35.602 --> 00:04:37.660
I thought it was gonna be super
similar to the metal thing.

00:04:37.660 --> 00:04:39.130
I'm like, okay, it's
gonna be a lot of work.

00:04:39.130 --> 00:04:42.270
Okay, gotta dig in, but
then hearing actually

00:04:42.270 --> 00:04:44.650
goth rock music, it puts me in an era.

00:04:44.650 --> 00:04:46.660
Early '80s, it sounds exactly like that,

00:04:46.660 --> 00:04:48.910
where the synths were long-y.

00:04:48.910 --> 00:04:51.120
- I don't know if this
is a thing throughout,

00:04:51.120 --> 00:04:53.250
but a lot of open intervals.

00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:57.030
Even if we're not dealing
with unstable harmony,

00:04:57.030 --> 00:04:59.858
like an augmented chord,

00:04:59.858 --> 00:05:01.130
(plays piano notes)

00:05:01.130 --> 00:05:02.403
but still just,

00:05:03.780 --> 00:05:05.270
something about this open sound,

00:05:05.270 --> 00:05:07.580
it feels like it doesn't really need to

00:05:07.580 --> 00:05:10.840
resolve anywhere or go anywhere,

00:05:10.840 --> 00:05:13.535
but it also doesn't
feel like it's settled.

00:05:13.535 --> 00:05:16.360
(playing piano)

00:05:16.360 --> 00:05:18.211
- If you had to resolve that,

00:05:18.211 --> 00:05:19.631
if you had to resolve
that, where would you go?

00:05:19.631 --> 00:05:22.798
(playing piano notes)

00:05:24.609 --> 00:05:25.442
(laughing)

00:05:25.442 --> 00:05:27.490
- You don't need to go anywhere.

00:05:27.490 --> 00:05:29.570
- Okay, so we know
where the music started.

00:05:29.570 --> 00:05:31.460
Can we assume that the fashion and style

00:05:31.460 --> 00:05:32.850
began about the same time?

00:05:32.850 --> 00:05:34.840
- It may have begun much earlier.

00:05:34.840 --> 00:05:37.300
Goth was directly inspired
by the romanticism

00:05:37.300 --> 00:05:39.950
of both the Romantic and Victorian era,

00:05:39.950 --> 00:05:42.090
drawing some of its earliest inspirations

00:05:42.090 --> 00:05:44.200
from Mary Shelley's Frankenstein,

00:05:44.200 --> 00:05:47.500
the writings of Edgar
Allan Poe and Bram Stoker,

00:05:47.500 --> 00:05:49.890
but oddly enough, the
beginnings of the goth style

00:05:49.890 --> 00:05:51.330
may have actually had its roots

00:05:51.330 --> 00:05:53.990
in the Victorian tuberculosis epidemic.

00:05:53.990 --> 00:05:56.190
The gaunt and pale look of those affected

00:05:56.190 --> 00:05:59.010
by the fatal disease
was often romanticized,

00:05:59.010 --> 00:06:02.230
influencing the mid-1800s
perceptions of beauty,

00:06:02.230 --> 00:06:05.230
which were later adopted by
the emerging goth culture.

00:06:05.230 --> 00:06:07.960
- Horror cinema also
inspired gothic style.

00:06:07.960 --> 00:06:10.990
Silent film star Theda Bara
was nicknamed the Vamp,

00:06:10.990 --> 00:06:13.020
and French actress Sarah
Bernhardt was known

00:06:13.020 --> 00:06:14.730
for sleeping in a coffin.

00:06:14.730 --> 00:06:17.330
Later, there was pinup model Bettie Page,

00:06:17.330 --> 00:06:21.690
Vampira actress Maila Nurmi,
and of course, TV's Elvira.

00:06:21.690 --> 00:06:23.920
- [Nahre] Goth was further
cemented in popular culture

00:06:23.920 --> 00:06:27.910
in 1976 with Anne Rice's
Interview With the Vampire,

00:06:27.910 --> 00:06:31.000
a novel believed to have also
heavily influenced the genre.

00:06:31.000 --> 00:06:34.700
- But now, back to Siouxsie
Sioux, the godmother of goth.

00:06:34.700 --> 00:06:37.580
Her raven hair and signature
eye makeup was more influenced

00:06:37.580 --> 00:06:40.259
by ancient Egyptians than the Victorians,

00:06:40.259 --> 00:06:42.000
but once the style was out the gate,

00:06:42.000 --> 00:06:43.920
it took on an afterlife of its own.

00:06:43.920 --> 00:06:45.690
- The goth culture as we know it today

00:06:45.690 --> 00:06:48.390
further developed in 1982 with the opening

00:06:48.390 --> 00:06:50.620
of the London goth club, The Batcave.

00:06:50.620 --> 00:06:52.220
The club inspired the spooky scene

00:06:52.220 --> 00:06:54.540
with regular performances from Bauhaus,

00:06:54.540 --> 00:06:57.580
Siouxsie and the Banshees,
as well as many others.

00:06:57.580 --> 00:06:59.580
Soon, goth spread across the globe,

00:06:59.580 --> 00:07:02.453
infecting many with its
dark music and fashion.

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:06.370
Are you ready to embrace your inner goth?

00:07:06.370 --> 00:07:07.203
- Let's get it.

00:07:08.830 --> 00:07:09.710
I'm with it.

00:07:09.710 --> 00:07:10.665
- Like this?

00:07:10.665 --> 00:07:11.498
(snorting)

00:07:11.498 --> 00:07:12.450
- I got a bass at the crib,

00:07:12.450 --> 00:07:14.765
and so I could definitely lay down.

00:07:14.765 --> 00:07:15.598
- Okay.

00:07:15.598 --> 00:07:16.431
(clapping)

00:07:16.431 --> 00:07:20.423
Yes, I'm all for LA playing bass guitar.

00:07:21.310 --> 00:07:23.774
- Tempo-wise, even if we have,

00:07:23.774 --> 00:07:26.370
(ticking)

00:07:26.370 --> 00:07:28.713
going up top, we could have,

00:07:29.680 --> 00:07:31.130
boom, boom, cah.

00:07:32.830 --> 00:07:33.663
- Yes!

00:07:35.290 --> 00:07:37.180
- It's still driving, it's still marching,

00:07:37.180 --> 00:07:38.940
but it's like you're walking,

00:07:38.940 --> 00:07:40.080
you're not walking down a sunny street.

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:41.180
You're walkin' in the woods.

00:07:41.180 --> 00:07:43.910
- That kinda has to just sound like

00:07:43.910 --> 00:07:48.320
we're getting in a dark
crevasse in our head.

00:07:48.320 --> 00:07:49.580
- [LA] In the mind, okay.

00:07:49.580 --> 00:07:51.740
- I want to add some textures.

00:07:51.740 --> 00:07:54.020
There has to be that fuzz.

00:07:54.020 --> 00:07:55.530
- They twisted it up, some way some how.

00:07:55.530 --> 00:07:57.700
There's a lot of sound
design with those synths.

00:07:57.700 --> 00:07:59.947
- Okay, I think we have a plan.

00:07:59.947 --> 00:08:02.864
(heavy bass music)

00:08:15.750 --> 00:08:17.980
- Wanna know more about
creatures of the night?

00:08:17.980 --> 00:08:20.130
Well head over to Monstrum, a PBS show

00:08:20.130 --> 00:08:22.600
all about monsters, myths, and folkore

00:08:22.600 --> 00:08:24.300
hosted by our friend Dr. Z,

00:08:24.300 --> 00:08:26.695
an expert of all things gothic.

00:08:26.695 --> 00:08:28.945
(clicking)

00:08:33.547 --> 00:08:35.964
(shimmering)

